# THE BOUNDARY OF THE COLLISION REFUSAL, from the side the BACKEND does not have to hold.
#
# `Comparer<Int?>` and `Comparer<Long?>` are one `Comparer<object>` after carrier-argument erasure, and a type
# cannot implement one CLR interface twice — so this looks like the supertype twin of the method/constructor
# collision. It is not, because the Kotlin FRONTEND already refuses it: two instantiations of one head are
# inconsistent type arguments, whatever the arguments are. Two DIFFERENT heads cannot erase onto one head, so the
# supertype collision is unreachable and bir2cir deliberately carries no check for it (a backend check there would
# be unreachable code pretending to be a safety net). This case pins that boundary: if the frontend ever started
# accepting the pair, this file would compile and the gate would say so.
type parameter 'T' of 'interface Comparer<T> : Any' has inconsistent values
a supertype appears twice
